## Structure (CQRS)

Each module splits reads and writes into separate paths:

```
src/
├── features/<module>/           ← per-feature vertical slice
│   ├── commands/                 ← write path (mutate state)
│   ├── queries/                  ← read path (query state)
│   ├── domain/                   ← entity, repository port, events
│   ├── infrastructure/           ← controller, repository impl, bus
│   └── dto/                      ← HTTP request/response shapes
```

**Write path:** Controller → Command → CommandBus → Handler → Repository
**Read path:**  Controller → Query → QueryBus → Handler → Repository

The controller never touches the repository directly — everything flows through the bus.
Each handler is a single-responsibility class: one command = one handler, one query = one handler.

Repository implementations are in-memory by default. To connect a real database, write a new
implementation of the repository interface and swap it in the module's DI wiring — since
handlers only depend on the interface, nothing else needs to change.

